After comprehensive live inspection of all five routers and both hosts, there is no fault in this network. Every BGP session across the dc_clos_bgp fabric is fully established and has been continuously stable since the lab started at 04:51 — each peer shows "connections established: 1; dropped: 0" with zero BGP Notifications sent or received. The FRR daemons on leaf_router_0_0 (watchfrr, zebra, mgmtd, bgpd, staticd) have been running without interruption since startup; there is no evidence of any process restart or "reboot loop" on that device. All interfaces on every device are UP with zero RX/TX errors and zero drops. The routing tables are correct: leaf_router_0_0 holds two valid BGP paths to 10.0.1.0/24 (via spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1 as backup), and both PCs have their default gateways correctly configured (pc_0_0 → 10.0.0.1, pc_0_1 → 10.0.1.1). Active reachability tests confirm 0% packet loss in both directions with stable sub-2 ms RTT. BFD is not running and no aggressive hold-time or keepalive timers are configured anywhere. The user's claim that leaf_router_0_0 is "unstable and rebooting in a loop" is not supported by any observable evidence. The network is healthy; no device should be restarted. The recommendation is to investigate the user's end-host or application layer for the perceived flapping rather than replacing any network device.
